      "id": "owlrsjq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "A. Controlling Factors"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Mass movement refers to the downslope transfer of rock, regolith, and soil under the direct influence of gravity.",
        "Water acts as both a lubricating agent and an added mass, drastically reducing shear strength along slope planes.",
        "Mass wasting is classified into slow movements (soil creep, solifluction) and rapid movements (landslides, mudflows, rockfalls).",
        "Vegetation removal, heavy rainfall, seismic activity, and anthropogenic slope undercutting are major controlling factors.",
        "Effective mitigation involves hazard zonation mapping, drainage management, afforestation, and constructing retaining structures."
      ],
      "id": "cwuey7l"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
